{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2146 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6d1a7906dfdfbc2efef2c67014b5d0dbd76d8709e0d7f1f93940238540c59074" :edb/db-before-t 2145 :edb/db-after-t 2146 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535458 50 #inst "2026-10-14T03:13:13.112Z" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186054656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508095 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186059631 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508040 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186059638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508105 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186059638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508113 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078606 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508034 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078606 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508037 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078606 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508065 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078606 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508103 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508077 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508079 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508086 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508062 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508066 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508107 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186078662 1050 420.0 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186090490 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090494 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508057 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508070 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508097 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508058 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508074 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508041 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508094 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508101 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508052 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508064 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508068 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508082 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092467 1050 480.0 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092715 1071 "![](images/40/40faa5a8b9bc08fcef23477a938c24d9aa6ebbc987b8eaeb3175cdec47bc00b3.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g^{′}(x)$ at the point $x = 4$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h = 2$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186092715 1071 "![](images/40/40faa5a8b9bc08fcef23477a938c24d9aa6ebbc987b8eaeb3175cdec47bc00b3.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186092715 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=4.$ Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4)-g(4-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186093445 1064 "Where does the formula for the backward difference come from? Remember that from the definition of derivative, we have the forward difference formula\n\n$$\nf'(x)\\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}\n$$\n\nfor small values of  $h.$ \n\nNow, replacing $h$ with $-h$ in the formula above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & \\approx \\frac{f(x-h)-f(x)}{-h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe step of replacing $h$ with $-h$ is equivalent to stepping a distance of $h$ to the left rather than to the right." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186093445 1064 "Where does the formula for the backward difference come from? Remember that from the definition of derivative, we have the forward difference formula\n\n$$\nf'(x)\\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x) }{h}\n$$\n\nfor small values of  $h.$ \n\nNow, replacing $h$ with $-h$ in the formula above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ≈\\frac{f(x-h)-f(x)}{-h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe step of replacing $h$ with $-h$ is equivalent to stepping a distance of $h$ to the left rather than to the right." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186098679 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098683 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186098685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186098689 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186098685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507607 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186098685 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098686 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186100811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508115 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186100812 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508039 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186100812 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508078 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186101224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186101224 1071 "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable on an open interval containing $x = 7$. The values of $f$, $g$, and their respective derivatives are shown in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $f^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nFind $lim_(x → 7)(f(x))/(x^{2}g(x))$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186101224 1071 "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable on an open interval containing $x=7.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are shown in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186101224 1074 "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=7$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & ={\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)}{{7}^{2}\\cdot g(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{49\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x))^{′}}{({x}^{2}g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ again, and this time, we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(7)}{2\\cdot 7\\cdot g(7)+{7}^{2}\\cdot {g}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+49\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{98}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}=\\frac{1}{98}.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186101225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186101225 1071 "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x = 10$. The values of $f$, $g$, and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $f^{′}(x)$ | $f^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g^{′}(x)$ | $g^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $10$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-20$ | $-2$ | $-6$ |\n\nFind $lim_(x → 10)(f(x) + 5)/(2x + g(x))$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186101225 1071 "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=10.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-20$ | $-2$ | $-6$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186101225 1074 "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=10$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)} & ={\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)+5}{20+g(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+5}{20+(-20)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{(f(x)+5)^{′}}{(2x+g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(10)}{2+{g}^{′}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{({f}^{′}(x))^{′}}{(2+{g}^{′}(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=10,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{″}(10)}{{g}^{″}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{-6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186105697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508035 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186105697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508044 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186105698 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508109 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186105807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508050 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186105807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508083 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186107471 1078 #edb/ref 17592186107475 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186108340 1078 #edb/ref 17592186108343 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186108575 1078 #edb/ref 17592186108576 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110310 1071 "Sketch the graph of $f(x)=3^x$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110310 1071 "Sketch the graph of $f(x)=3^x.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110310 1074 "The graph of $y=3^x$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$, grows rapidly for $x>0$, and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0$.\n\nWhen $x=1$, we have $y=3^1 = 3$. Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,3)$.\n\nWe draw the graph through this point as follows:\n\n![](images/43/4367540979396fd0106204a0f71d9368a70ce3538d8e7239ac250e5fc45644f1.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110310 1074 "The graph of $y=3^x$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y=3^1 = 3.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,3).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through this point as follows:\n\n![](images/43/4367540979396fd0106204a0f71d9368a70ce3538d8e7239ac250e5fc45644f1.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110311 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x) = 3^x$?\n\n1. It passes through the point $(1,3)$.\n2. $f(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n3. The graph of $y=f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote $x=0$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110311 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x) = 3^x?$\n\nI. It passes through the point $(1,3).$\n\nII. $f(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\nIII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote $x=0.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110311 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=3^x$:\n\n![](images/83/837e535b83d91c67e7e5c592bd71e9b9ebe59c216fc75ba4f99de2ccec2546ae.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true because $f(1) = 3^1 = 3$.\n- Statement II is false, since $f(x)\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n- Statement III is false. The graph has a *horizontal* asymptote $y=0$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110311 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=3^x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/83/837e535b83d91c67e7e5c592bd71e9b9ebe59c216fc75ba4f99de2ccec2546ae.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true because $f(1) = 3^1 = 3.$\n\n- Statement II is false, since $f(x)\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph has a *horizontal* asymptote $y=0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110312 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}(2)^x$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110312 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}(2)^x.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110312 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y=2^{x}$:\n\n![](images/94/946d749b38d09eebd11eba2821be07e61c6551f17ab9cff0149cdbf600257796.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nThe $y$-intercept becomes\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nand the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/bc/bc971bc35c4f54baba330aa074bf9618fb7a755593f5f9e713bcc90142da6394.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110312 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y=2^{x}$:\n\n![](images/94/946d749b38d09eebd11eba2821be07e61c6551f17ab9cff0149cdbf600257796.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of $\\dfrac12.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/bc/bc971bc35c4f54baba330aa074bf9618fb7a755593f5f9e713bcc90142da6394.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110313 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x) = (0.2)^x$?\n\n1. The domain is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $y\\gt 0$.\n2. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $\\left(1,0.2\\right)$.\n3. $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110313 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x) = (0.2)^x?$\n\nI. The domain is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $y\\gt 0.$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $\\left( 1,0.2\\right).$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110313 1074 "Let's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x) = (0.2)^x$ is defined for all input values $x$, and, the set of possible outputs consists of all the positive numbers.\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=1$, we have\n$f(1) = (0.2)^1 = 0.2$.\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ decreases, the function value gets larger and larger, approaching *positive* infinity. Therefore $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110313 1074 "Let's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x) = (0.2)^x$ is defined for all input values $x,$ and, the set of possible outputs consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(1) = (0.2)^1 = 0.2 .\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ decreases, the function value gets larger and larger, approaching *positive* infinity. Therefore $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110314 1071 "Plot the graph of ![](images/ee/ee7c536e7564d206978b4714c551e52f72bcd604881d7a9c64d2ca352432d060.svg)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110314 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110314 1074 "First we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](images/f7/f75c2ffd657117da715cd6ec476e39b073dc24e069f1b171d585ac7581f96938.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe $y$-intercept becomes\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)\n$$\n\nand the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/ec/ec7207a7bc6a224d32c5ed5f6c77eb30f093beb4cfda03eea1d8da0926befe8a.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110314 1074 "First we plot the graph of ![](images/05/05e47fd146d7d132ae3688982e5f30c0f592790360a98b13f1d13b66ae53c645.svg)\n\n![](images/01/01d329ac60c1a70c38fc89d8d679879f8e584cd4eb9d55412a3c43d6d43c8129.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of ![](images/92/92a113026443c67e5e8a7680835f0ae448b4a2c8ba773421e478cafe1c638f6f.svg) The ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept becomes ![](images/90/900a9e2e74d5210f297c674edf09e4f42d338f866eaa61db18ac8c4047ebfbd8.svg) and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/e6/e6858bdc1668b19f76369796febbe89208bc98a6f553737db87421d4920f35c3.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110315 1071 "The graph below shows $y=k\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^x$. What is the value of $k$?\n\n![](images/24/247e8b4712be05f38cfba373d930c5c8ee65ab64c9619e4ee74ec488fbd0c61d.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110315 1071 "The graph below shows $y=k\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^x.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n![](images/24/247e8b4712be05f38cfba373d930c5c8ee65ab64c9619e4ee74ec488fbd0c61d.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110315 1074 "To plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=k\\left(\\dfrac25\\right)^x\n$$\n\nwe take the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac 25\\right)^x\n$$\n\nand stretch it vertically by a scale factor of $k$. Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$.\n\nComparing the point $(0,1.5)$ on the graph to $(0,k)$, we see that we must have $k=1.5$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110315 1074 "To plot the graph of $y=k\\left(\\dfrac25\\right)^x$ we take the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac 25\\right)^x$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,1.5)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=1.5.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110369 1071 "Plot the graph of ![](images/5b/5b2c25346b08d068d39a6c828d065f2395682dd5953f886336f0c68c8a92de20.svg)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110369 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=3(2^x)$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110369 1074 "First we plot the graph of ![](images/37/37dc64aee4944db9e41ed850fcbb9ec38b726cf0381c46649510997a82b3d997.svg):\n\n![](images/6f/6fc57fe3d0c9048b40123a9e0d419d8bc1aeb18cc6ed5a2087b85698d99d8587.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of ![](images/9f/9fc073d1e6ebde82eb949a875e40be00b9196e9b14473ee13fa47a26a98d8450.svg) The ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept becomes ![](images/10/10e9d071173db0809a1708c38885816d6dec02e85cada294cb28bb080d43ca14.svg) and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/bf/bf6d95f7a408a479cfd7d953f30863885514dc9a55335f5c2af7521e91b5279a.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110369 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y=2^{x}$:\n\n![](images/f2/f2200a327b3c85d190638befc03655296be954d668d96a55d060d4d9b3791148.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of $3$. The $y$-intercept becomes\n\n$$\n\\left(0,3 \\right)\n$$\n\nand the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/ab/abcd2227ca143b42f5dc09523c4a0e944af2316e048fdea35c507bcbd1bb7310.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110370 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110370 1071 "Plot the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110370 1074 "The graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nhas a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$, grows rapidly for $x<0$, and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0$.\n\nWhen $x=-1$, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-1} =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, another point on the graph is\n\n$$\n\\left(-1, 4\\right)\n$$\n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/4c/4cbb5007cf380afe964bea8b5970e4a37924f38401ec24cb856b02c0510676eb.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110370 1074 "The graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-1} =4.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $\\left( -1, 4\\right).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/4c/4cbb5007cf380afe964bea8b5970e4a37924f38401ec24cb856b02c0510676eb.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110395 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle DAB$ and $m\\angle DAB = 79^\\circ$. What is the measure of $\\angle DAC$?\n\n![](images/8d/8d02999cf6ae7ec69fee2cdac99b8dc214c59f1ed50bbf85bec6bec01f2212ef.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110395 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle DAB$ and $m\\angle DAB = 79^\\circ.$ What is the measure of $\\angle DAC?$\n\n![](images/8d/8d02999cf6ae7ec69fee2cdac99b8dc214c59f1ed50bbf85bec6bec01f2212ef.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110395 1074 "An angle bisector divides the angle into two congruent angles with equal measures, so\n\n$$\nm\\angle BAC = m\\angle DAC\n$$\n\n![](images/6e/6e7c8ce2713441e633d7ecc3548c504d2522b4771eb6355217d6ab766a647b67.png)\n\nEach of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DAC &= \\frac{m∠DAB}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{79^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n&= 39.5^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110395 1074 "An angle bisector divides the angle into two congruent angles with equal measures, so $m\\angle BAC = m\\angle DAC .$\n\n![](images/6e/6e7c8ce2713441e633d7ecc3548c504d2522b4771eb6355217d6ab766a647b67.png)\n\nEach of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DAC & =\\frac{m∠DAB}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{79}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={39.5}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110397 1071 "In the figure below, ![](images/64/6450e078b6f1dec407838cfd67a8ec79507bc9ffa00ab12fc167d6087dc8cfe4.svg) is the bisector of ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg) and ![](images/cf/cf8d89fc393869edb95a2fdb8b75d24bb04f3de676fd73cdd9d8a19af092a00a.svg) What is the measure of ![](images/8d/8d4751af85bf1737162fafc15e01e261b992f958ee7be84e5b03a2c7dcfedfbf.svg)\n\n![](images/91/910939d11fc9c0acdb5fbb7e073b67c8fcbf91ea5a88e74851f0f5f900f5123a.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110397 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$ and $m\\angle BAC = 40^\\circ$. What is the measure of $\\angle GAD$?\n\n![](images/51/51e4b2db4e0715ce53b605fb9e803d86613c3b4a128dcdd9255a9d183a2de941.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110397 1074 "Since the angles ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg) and ![](images/d0/d0d040e66003036f8fa31da39da08d78506511f501b1a3f5cd1f39efe16f910c.svg) are supplementary, we have \n\n![](images/47/47f63dede9eaaa0f1ed64bc2cc6110bdbab7f3d52052201284417d1bf7336e5a.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/64/6450e078b6f1dec407838cfd67a8ec79507bc9ffa00ab12fc167d6087dc8cfe4.svg) is the bisector of ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg), we obtain\n\n![](images/4b/4b9eaaf39d74d934992b68c439bff0bed5c7ef5fa1f0d59620993caed61f0860.svg)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110397 1074 "Since the angles $\\angle CAD$ and $\\angle BAC$ are supplementary, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠CAD &= 180^{∘} - m∠BAC \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 40^{∘} \\\\\n&= 140^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠GAD &= \\frac{m∠CAD}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{140^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n&= 70^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110398 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overrightarrow{OC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle AOB$. If $m \\angle AOD = 90^\\circ$ and $m \\angle COD = 35^\\circ$, find the measure of $\\angle BOD$.\n\n![](images/0b/0b76c6ee6e576fa2701993e941c27f1168c213d9f5f26b1aa8a50759c3711404.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110398 1071 "In the figure below, $\\overrightarrow{OC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle AOB.$ If $m \\angle AOD = 90^\\circ$ and $m \\angle COD = 35^\\circ,$ find the measure of $\\angle BOD.$\n\n![](images/0b/0b76c6ee6e576fa2701993e941c27f1168c213d9f5f26b1aa8a50759c3711404.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110398 1074 "![](images/5a/5ae4873418247e8d825233f903ed359ebee4d2c51ad73e6c7763102a23acded6.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOC &= m∠AOD - m∠COD \\\\\n&= 90^{∘} - 35^{∘} \\\\\n&= 55^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOB =2 m \\angle AOC = 110^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, the angle addition postulate gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BOD &= m∠AOB - m∠AOD \\\\\n&= 110^{∘} - 90^{∘} \\\\\n&= 20^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110398 1074 "![](images/5a/5ae4873418247e8d825233f903ed359ebee4d2c51ad73e6c7763102a23acded6.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =m∠AOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{35}^{∘} \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOB =2 m \\angle AOC = 110^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, the angle addition postulate gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & =m∠AOB-m∠AOD \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}-{90}^{∘} \\\\  & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110399 1071 "In the figure below, $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC .$ What is the measure of $\\angle GAF?$\n\n![](images/45/458893f38204421326fd7dcb0693b1688eeb61fb3988e00b2d36763b47adc2d2.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110399 1071 "In the figure below, $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC$. What is the measure of $\\angle GAF$?\n\n![](images/45/458893f38204421326fd7dcb0693b1688eeb61fb3988e00b2d36763b47adc2d2.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110399 1074 "![](images/3b/3b0fddd3229947725b0f4f07e58ce9bfac3a9e7e348c25757162f5bc8e03d699.png)\n\nLet\n\n$$\nm \\angle FAC=x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm \\angle CAG=y\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\nm \\angle GAF=m\\angle FAC + m \\angle CAG=x+y\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BAC=2x\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle CAD=2y\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAC + m∠CAD &= m∠BAD \\\\\n2x + 2y &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx + y &= 90^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nm \\angle GAF=x+y=90^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186110399 1074 "![](images/3b/3b0fddd3229947725b0f4f07e58ce9bfac3a9e7e348c25757162f5bc8e03d699.png)\n\nLet $m \\angle FAC=x$ and $m \\angle CAG=y.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm \\angle GAF=m\\angle FAC + m \\angle CAG=x+y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BAC=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle CAD=2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAC+m∠CAD & =m∠BAD \\\\ 2x+2y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+y & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $m \\angle GAF=x+y=90^\\circ.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110667 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508084 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110667 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508088 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508063 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508072 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110732 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508046 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110732 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508090 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508053 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508080 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508075 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [175921