49860122e051b8427e4342a829f150aa3b30e6e.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. $f(1)=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}=\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{7}.$ Therefore, the point $(1,\\frac{3}{7})$ lies on the curve.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111100 1071 "Which of the following represents the graph of $y = 0.5(9^{x})$?" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186111100 1071 "Which of the following represents the graph of $y=0.5({9}^{x})?$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111100 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y={9}^{x}$:\n\n![](images/27/270006d7d2e7d7cab172ba586df796af8b78d9b00157f439bf65cc0e151ed13e.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of $0.5.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,0.5),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3d0e0db5029b92622d28c478c6a8d3ee02dee21fe3fa1f82965838b22d07f543.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111140 1071 "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $y = (\\frac{3}{5})^{x}$?" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186111140 1071 "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}?$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111140 1074 "The graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y=(0.6)^{-1}=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,\\frac{5}{3}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/0c/0c5bf6531bf352da158771f92eaab5a3974ccd623d1bf29dbe3ecba287f61d5d.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111825 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x$. First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$.\n2. The graph is increasing.\n3. For $x>0$, the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n4. For $x<0$, the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0$. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n5. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$, and the range is $y>0$.\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1$. These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186111825 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x.$ First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1\\. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1).$\n\n2\\. The graph is increasing.\n\n3\\. For $x>0,$ the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty.$\n\n4\\. For $x<0,$ the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n5\\. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty , \\infty )$, and the range is $y>0.$\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1.$ These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111826 1064 "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/68/68dcb337cb0927d43403eebeb21c0a54dc46a136f7cd21ce821d9bfc325c748e.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)  It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/70/7052037576cfaf29807f0ad332100b9929a72806846219da2599c8f911500d87.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg)\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111826 1064 "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve $y=2(3^x)$ represents a vertical scaling of $y=3^x$ by a factor of $2$. It takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=3^x$ and multiplies them by $2$. This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/49/49c2136298e6fbf1adcc4c24ddef6d3daf47367d4d3f15dc18c476dff086087a.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186111827 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of ![](images/d0/d0a7418e3803197d07b85f03b206b83bed1c4d451397ae953b05634db9a9f829.svg) First, we create a table of values: \n\n| ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) | ![](images/78/78d4465d85b55cee3832a9b598a2564b6a3fa9a072de8a1fb4b40ca884079670.svg) | ![](images/63/63df422d619ca962ca5a69bc7d25dd8831269b92fa5d641dcb960819d3868a9a.svg) | ![](images/66/66ef4b5796888b4025ee2d8b3d516ec80741b341db9389696b6e77a4af5208d8.svg) | ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253e