11a9803605bd54156d90d3fcc6ff4639dd179a5fb024bcffaa1.svg) Symbolically, we can write ![](images/43/43a6d025efce6b330ba7907d1daa54ad58cf04feee07adedad66d4c806b5f88d.svg) as ![](images/f9/f9b318487299434d45096742eaad8ea148abb88c27b718c1bc0983f927dc9911.svg)\n\n5\\. The domain is ![](images/04/043681a7084203dcb8609f78989cadb8f0bcd701978361940d93ef850da95a14.svg), and the range is ![](images/95/95d0bc38cd8737723521d2a1846cb71a0dba33d3df26bd73c18acbb650fc8b95.svg)\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve ![](images/90/90517bb7bc12cfec9d00d88592bbda7572f4a0c5cbcf4f66049311d42f20f6ca.svg) with ![](images/e2/e276af13a21df7f4755de7b18da1a10ca590bbdf3387515efa0896f6cddb79cf.svg) These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/0f/0f8e7cc0db94f5f51bc83404351a7708171fdce50e2946b51263885e4230ecef.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111827 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=0.5^x$. First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y = 0.5^x$ | $8$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ | $0.125$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/b1/b17b1e0f554b8da98f46b89265cbec3be2fab1a40b6c04ef03f5b4cec94ac3d8.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$.\n2. The graph is decreasing.\n3. For $x<0$, the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ decreases. Symbolically, we can write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n4. For $x>0$, the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ increases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has a horizontal asymptote $y=0$. Symbolically, we can write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n5. The domain is $x\\in\\left(-\\infty, \\infty\\right)$, and the range is $y>0$.\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $0 < a < 1$. These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/6c/6c5e94fdb76c1af54dcd1cd8d44bb532ab5c69a305465282b62c28ef2b54ab75.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186111828 1064 "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve\n\n$$\ny=2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nby a factor of $2$.\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nand multiplies them by $2$. This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/36/3654463dd3914bb65ffe58be20cdf2bc4cd19e5a51ac479f4aaca50e2709a214.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$.\n\nOn the other hand, the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\left(2^x\\right)\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n$y = 2^x$\nby a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y = 2^x$ and multiplies them by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nThis has the effect of **compressing** the curve downwards.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched or compressed exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186111828 1064 "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/d5/d59881254ee1a66445dcf2697e8af4426cbe9d219443d5f40ef2aefc426d598b.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/fb/fb1255ff2ad78ef15b03968cb806543d4f29f1029680068f76c913bb4cab96ad.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\nIt takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/fb/fb1255ff2ad78ef15b03968cb806543d4f29f1029680068f76c913bb4cab96ad