186045417 true] [17592186111833 1064 "The curve\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{3}(3^x)\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of $y=3^x$ by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=3^x$ and multiplies them by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThis has the effect of squashing, or **compressing**, the curve downwards.\n\n![](images/e3/e3ae2f177dd60050dd4c4035c66ebfc46d1028eed74f1d23178ddaae35d35378.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nIn general for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186111833 1064 "The curve ![](images/e9/e910a01694746b83e1662e73779d7e758399e2d0bdf7441bbd8221a37f278b17.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) This has the effect of squashing, or **compressing**, the curve downwards.\n\n![](images/68/685e360ed7bcd7975aa705d192ef487af17688bb113b20e10f881a0bdf7f46b7.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/97/97a51f546ed69cb656e8a811f3a4acf3f6dacce8e9ed7cac63419ad63f0497d1.svg)\n\nIn general for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186111842 1064 "An angle **bisector** is a line that divides an angle into two equal parts.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of the angle $\\angle BAD$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf65e915f4fc26b8d87199f46ea0bc514195d857890983d31aa2d73eb38a811.png)\n\nThe resulting angles $\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are congruent (they have the same measure)." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186111842 1064 "An angle **bisector** is a line that divides an angle into two equal parts. \n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of the angle $\\angle BAD.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bf65e915f4fc26b8d87199f46ea0bc514195d857890983d31aa2d73eb38a811.png)\n\nThe resulting angles $\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are congruent (they have the same measure)." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186121982 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121983 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124313 1074 "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is \n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =4{x}^{2}+8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/8} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0).$  Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124313 1074 "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is \n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =4{x}^{2}+8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/8} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0).$  Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186124457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508069 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124504 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508076 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124504 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508102 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124504 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508110 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124556 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508116 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124558 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508060 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124650 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508047 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124650 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508091 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124695 1050 600.0 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124696 1050 540.0 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121886 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186124882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121892 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186124897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124897 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $4(x - 1)^{2} + 25(y + 1)^{2} = 100(z - 2)$ and the plane\n$z = 7$ onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186124897 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $4(x-1)^{2}+25(y+1)^{2}=100(z-2)$ and the plane \n$z=7$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124897 1074 "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=7$, we substitute $z=7$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+25(y+1)^{2}=100(z-2) \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+25(y+1)^{2}=100(7-2) \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+25(y+1)^{2}=500 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{125}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{20} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{5})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $5\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124988 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & =9((x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186124988 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & =9((x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 1}.\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186125773 1064 "A **hyperboloid of one sheet** centered at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is a quadric surface whose equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of one sheet\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=1\n$$\n\nis shown below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/0d/0d97e5ac26685b49bcdb3155866bd34ba83bd758f02035b500d3f594fc5716c8.png)\n\n   \n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the coordinate axes are as follows:\n\n- the $x$-intercepts are $(\\pm1,0,0)$\n\n- the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\pm1,0)$\n\n- there are no $z$-intercepts" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186125773 1064 "A **hyperboloid of one sheet** centered at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is a quadric surface whose equation is given by\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =1,$$\n\nwhere $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of one sheet\n\n$$x^2+y^2-z^2=1$$\n\nis shown below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/0d/0d97e5ac26685b49bcdb3155866bd34ba83bd758f02035b500d3f594fc5716c8.png)\n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the coordinate axes are as follows:\n\n- the $x$-intercepts are $(\\pm1,0,0)$\n- the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\pm1,0)$\n- there are no $z$-intercepts" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186125774 1064 "Consider the quadric surface with the equation\n\n$$\nz = x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14e8d23a4bc649257c2f7351e0d2c1d62e39a8cd8eed1467e65853ee121b836e.png)\n\n \n\nThis surface is an example of an **elliptic paraboloid.**\n\n The **vertex** of this elliptic paraboloid is located at the origin, and its **axis of symmetry** is the $z$-axis. Note that the axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis whose vertex is at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nThese surfaces are called *elliptic* *paraboloids* because their traces in the $xy$-plane are *ellipses*, while their traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186125774 1064 "Consider the quadric surface with the equation\n\n$$z = x^2 + y^2$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14e8d23a4bc649257c2f7351e0d2c1d62e39a8cd8eed1467e65853ee121b836e.png)\n\nThis surface is an example of an **elliptic paraboloid.**\n\nThe **vertex** of this elliptic paraboloid is located at the origin, and its **axis of symmetry** is the $z$-axis. Note that the axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis whose vertex is at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$z - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nThese surfaces are called *elliptic* *paraboloids* because their traces in the $xy$-plane are *ellipses*, while their traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186125775 1064 "A **hyperboloid of two sheets** centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =-1.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This equation is similar to the general equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet. The only difference is the right-hand side is now $-1$ instead of $+1.$\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of two sheets \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=-1\n$$\n\nis represented below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/96/96ec4196c3e299d2e57cdb8a3a2a06094452bf05e2f66e43fc60c059077b305f.png)\n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,\\pm1).$ The surface does not intersect the $x$- or $y$-axes.\n\nCalculating the intercepts of a hyperboloid with the coordinate axes is done in the same way as we've seen before with spheres and ellipsoids. Let's see some examples.\n\nThere is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids of one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186125775 1064 "A **hyperboloid of two sheets** centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =-1.$$\n\n**Watch out!** This equation is similar to the general equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet. The only difference is the right-hand side is now $-1$ instead of $+1.$\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of two sheets\n\n$$x^2+y^2-z^2=-1$$\n\nis represented below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/96/96ec4196c3e299d2e57cdb8a3a2a06094452bf05e2f66e43fc60c059077b305f.png)\n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,\\pm1).$ The surface does not intersect the $x$- or $y$-axes.\n\nCalculating the intercepts of a hyperboloid with the coordinate axes is done in the same way as we've seen before with spheres and ellipsoids. Let's see some examples.\n\nThere is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids of one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet: $$\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$$\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets: $$\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186125776 1064 "Now consider the graph of the quadric surface\n\n$$\nz = x^2 -y^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f02c207742c737d00cdeabbd2cbba93a88a42521c41a2cc1e9f19d27ff55f20.png)\n\n \n\nThis surface is an example of a **hyperbolic paraboloid** whose **saddle point** is at the origin.\n\nMore generally, a hyperbolic paraboloid whose saddle point is at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ has the general equation\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2}- \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ constants. \n\nThis surface is called *hyperbolic* *paraboloid* because its traces in the $xy$-plane are *hyperbolas*, while its traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly.\n\nAnother property is that this surface mirrors itself across the planes $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Since these planes meet along the $z$-axis, that axis serves as the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperbolic paraboloid." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186125776 1064 "Now consider the graph of the quadric surface\n\n$$z = x^2 -y^2$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f02c207742c737d00cdeabbd2cbba93a88a42521c41a2cc1e9f19d27ff55f20.png)\n\nThis surface is an example of a **hyperbolic paraboloid** whose **saddle point** is at the origin.\n\nMore generally, a hyperbolic paraboloid whose saddle point is at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ has the general equation $$z - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2}- \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ constants.\n\nThis surface is called *hyperbolic* *paraboloid* because its traces in the $xy$-plane are *hyperbolas*, while its traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly.\n\nAnother property is that this surface mirrors itself across the planes $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Since these planes meet along the $z$-axis, that axis serves as the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperbolic paraboloid." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186125805 1064 "So far, we've encountered elliptic paraboloids whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. \nLet's now consider the cases when an elliptic paraboloid is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor an elliptic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows.\n\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis:\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/da/da37415ddd27764a719fecc5780ee91e6e8ad3f5ce877101b81d1a955c57eb6f.png)\n\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d1488d9f4767312795a84ce6441f64dd567223d1de4da66adbd6e39de9612a53.png)\n\nLikewise, for a hyperbolic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis. The two corresponding equations are\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}.\n$$\n\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis. The two corresponding equations are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nx = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186125805 1064 "So far, we've encountered elliptic paraboloids whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. Let's now consider the cases when an elliptic paraboloid is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor an elliptic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows.\n\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis: $$y = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}$$\n\n![](images/da/da37415ddd27764a719fecc5780ee91e6e8ad3f5ce877101b81d1a955c57eb6f.png)\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis: $$x = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}$$\n\n![](images/d1/d1488d9f4767312795a84ce6441f64dd567223d1de4da66adbd6e39de9612a53.png)\n\nLikewise, for a hyperbolic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis. The two corresponding equations are $$y = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}.$$\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis. The two corresponding equations are $$x = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186125877 1064 "The traces of a hyperboloid of one sheet are either ellipses or hyperbolas.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperboloid of one sheet given by\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=1.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle centered at the origin of radius $1$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/b3/b369958a609ba3861be5d32fbf3195b6feb241228f81e89bb116b29f80e38318.png)\n\n   \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $xz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/4c/4c77b8758877ac88a73dece8c52f3b9cb650c4176edad5db020b138d70e0393b.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $yz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/0d/0ddcfc2e78166fa801a46deccd30c4cc2ddc12e81ae2e81508b5e13a374c208f.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186125877 1064 "The traces of a hyperboloid of one sheet are either ellipses or hyperbolas.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperboloid of one sheet given by\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=1.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle centered at the origin of radius $1$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/b3/b369958a609ba3861be5d32fbf3195b6feb241228f81e89bb116b29f80e38318.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $xz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/4c/4c77b8758877ac88a73dece8c52f3b9cb650c4176edad5db020b138d70e0393b.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $yz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/0d/0ddcfc2e78166fa801a46deccd30c4cc2ddc12e81ae2e81508b5e13a374c208f.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186125879 1064 "Let's consider the graphs a typical one-sheet and two-sheet hyperboloids centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/45/45359d1c73d9cdc721068dbee0e27bedb8cde622bae0615c16862e4157b2baf3.png)\n\n   \n\nNotice that a hyperboloid of two sheets is defined for any $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ In fact, for any point $(x,y)$ in the $xy$-plane, there are exactly two points on the hyperboloid. \n\nOn the other hand, the graph of a hyperboloid of one sheet does not contain points over the central elliptical region (close to the axis of symmetry).\n\nFor example, given the hyperboloid of one sheet $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1,$ let's find the domain of $z = z(x,y).$\n\nFrom the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the *exterior* and boundary of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ for the hyperboloid $x^2+y^2-z^2 = 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\left \\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x^2 + y^2 \\geq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186125879 1064 "Let's consider the graphs a typical one-sheet and two-sheet hyperboloids centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/45/45359d1c73d9cdc721068dbee0e27bedb8cde622bae0615c16862e4157b2baf3.png)\n\nNotice that a hyperboloid of two sheets is defined for any $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ In fact, for any point $(x,y)$ in the $xy$-plane, there are exactly two points on the hyperboloid. \n\nOn the other hand, the graph of a hyperboloid of one sheet does not contain points over the central elliptical region (close to the axis of symmetry).\n\nFor example, given the hyperboloid of one sheet $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1,$ let's find the domain of $z = z(x,y).$\n\nFrom the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the *exterior* and boundary of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ for the hyperboloid $x^2+y^2-z^2 = 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\left \\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x^2 + y^2 \\geq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186125880 1064 "The traces of an elliptic paraboloid are either ellipses, parabolas, a single point, or the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the elliptic paraboloid given by\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/02/021dd988863021f564a0e85fb3db93030740059f2a50ade6504b3e12a5665372.png)\n\n \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ {x}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/9f/9f6cd0703b5d7b2458cbfd688d91f08a79752fa1ccd3616e5975f5c017aa7b42.png)\n\n \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ {y}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/89/89be283ed121d3e30b1913e4412b8d3556ed611ef2a9a9bfd686d0594409f5c9.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=-k$ for $k > 0$ is the empty set. This is because $z\\geq 0$ everywhere on the elliptic paraboloid.\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=k$ for $k > 0$ is an ellipse:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = k\n$$\n\n![](images/f1/f15e925431b0d2ccfd4afa703cad1bcb37b65a75e96fbeabcf4823703bf184e5.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186125880 1064 "The traces of an elliptic paraboloid are either ellipses, parabolas, a single point, or the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the elliptic paraboloid given by\n\n$$z=x^2+y^2.$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/02/021dd988863021f564a0e85fb3db93030740059f2a50ade6504b3e12a5665372.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ {x}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}$$ which is a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/9f/9f6cd0703b5d7b2458cbfd688d91f08a79752fa1ccd3616e5975f5c017aa7b42.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ {y}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}$$ which is a parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/89/89be283ed121d3e30b1913e4412b8d3556ed611ef2a9a9bfd686d0594409f5c9.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=-k$ for $k > 0$ is the empty set. This is because $z\\geq 0$ everywhere on the elliptic paraboloid.\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=k$ for $k > 0$ is an ellipse: $$x^2+y^2 = k$$\n\n![](images/f1/f15e925431b0d2ccfd4afa703cad1bcb37b65a75e96fbeabcf4823703bf184e5.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186125881 1064 "The traces of a hyperbolic paraboloid are either hyperbolas, parabolas, or intersecting lines.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperbolic paraboloid given by\n\n$$\nz=x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two intersecting lines in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9b/9b3fbb9d8945c5d9208a59d404e489e4a339082c602c18154010e3e53083b220.png)\n\n \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & ={x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/e8/e8e2b6306052e781fd149e4eebb04da79eddb44b5261b05b129f208ad523b70b.png)\n\n \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & =-{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/70/7010e4c26af60c6c27f66c93b805a2bac1e191c1646b5aac3a77f5dd3dadc64f.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular case, the **saddle point** is at the origin. It is a minimum point for the trace in the $xz$-plane but a maximum point for the trace in the $yz$-plane.\n\nFinally, note that the trace of our surface with the plane $z=k\\neq 0$ is a hyperbola:\n\n$$\nx^2-y^2 = k\n$$\n\n![](images/b9/b9c1baab2f9d85c2c415cae91908d5c908aa0b1cef1eb8b5901f7f3b78bf66b2.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186125881 1064 "The traces of a hyperbolic paraboloid are either hyperbolas, parabolas, or intersecting lines.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperbolic paraboloid given by\n\n$$z=x^2-y^2.$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm x\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to two intersecting lines in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9b/9b3fbb9d8945c5d9208a59d404e489e4a339082c602c18154010e3e53083b220.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & ={x}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to the parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/e8/e8e2b6306052e781fd149e4eebb04da79eddb44b5261b05b129f208ad523b70b.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & =-{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to the parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/70/7010e4c26af60c6c27f66c93b805a2bac1e191c1646b5aac3a77f5dd3dadc64f.png)\n\nIn this particular case, the **saddle point** is at the origin. It is a minimum point for the trace in the $xz$-plane but a maximum point for the trace in the $yz$-plane.\n\nFinally, note that the trace of our surface with the plane $z=k\\neq 0$ is a hyperbola: $$x^2-y^2 = k$$\n\n![](images/b9/b9c1baab2f9d85c2c415cae91908d5c908aa0b1cef1eb8b5901f7f3b78bf66b2.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186155541 1078 #edb/ref 17592186155542 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186157393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508043 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186157393 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508093 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186157770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186157770 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $R^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $4x_{3}$, where $x_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}$.\n$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186157770 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $4{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186157770 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & =x\\cdot {q}_{3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n4{x}_{3}=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186163643 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186163644 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186163645 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186163646 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186163647 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507652 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507832 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507894 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507923 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186163642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507935 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186163642 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507832 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186163654 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163657 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186166867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508059 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186167094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186167094 1071 "![](images/b5/b56ff4cb47d9b529762c6364eac2eed4b666c7ca9eab6f6ba7d3bc161387433e.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186167094 1071 "![](images/b5/b56ff4cb47d9b529762c6364eac2eed4b666c7ca9eab6f6ba7d3bc161387433e.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for the ellipse shown above." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186167094 1074 "Since our ellipse is centered at $(2,-1)$ and has semiaxes $3$ and $4,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-2)}{9}+\\frac{2(y+1)}{16}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2(x-2)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{16(x-2)}{9(y+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186167096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186167096 1071 "![](images/88/88bec66b7ece961f2f0fc4083ce317f2bdc414e0a219d61bbf9bf73ae667ca0f.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186167096 1071 "![](images/88/88bec66b7ece961f2f0fc4083ce317f2bdc414e0a219d61bbf9bf73ae667ca0f.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186167096 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-2,2).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2$ and $y=2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2} & =4p(-2) \\\\ 4 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179477 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = 2x-1$ for $x \\in (-1,1]$. What is its range?" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186179477 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = 2x-1$ for $x \\in (-1,1].$ What is its range?" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179477 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/32/32925e498e936298ada23ba1c560b992b7dc2dce0e39fd6479bf6ba5ce2cb545.png)\n\nThen, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values on the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-3$. This occurs at the point $(-1,-3)$. However, the graph does not include this point.\n- The largest $y$-value is $1$. This occurs at the point $(1,1)$.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-3$ and $1$, including $1$ but not including $-3$.\n\nTherefore, the range is\n\n$$\n-3 < y \\leq 1\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\n-3 < f(x) \\leq 1\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186179477 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/32/32925e498e936298ada23ba1c560b992b7dc2dce0e39fd6479bf6ba5ce2cb545.png)\n\nThen, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values on the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-3.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,-3).$ However, the graph does not include this point.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs at the point $(1,1).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-3$ and $1,$ including $1$ but not including $-3.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-3 < y \\leq 1,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-3 < f(x) \\leq 1.\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179478 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)$, shown below?\n\n![](images/88/880925c00705275ef8630444a08053bc6ca2c79b2e4754a09d0bd7a2af059f32.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186179478 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown below?\n\n![](images/88/880925c00705275ef8630444a08053bc6ca2c79b2e4754a09d0bd7a2af059f32.png)" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179478 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-1$. This occurs when $x\\to \\infty$. However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $\\infty$, but not including $-1$.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-1 \\lt y \\lt \\infty$, which can also be written as $-1 \\lt f(x) \\lt \\infty$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186179478 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-1.$ This occurs when $x\\to \\infty.$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $\\infty,$ but not including $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-1 \\lt  y \\lt \\infty,$ which can also be written as  $-1 \\lt f(x) \\lt \\infty.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179479 1071 "![](images/a2/a277f71617ce2dceae7ee97017655c4c27e57a9bb22020995905760e53bf7720.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x)$, shown above?" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186179479 1071 "![](images/a2/a277f71617ce2dceae7ee97017655c4c27e57a9bb22020995905760e53bf7720.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179479 1074 "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-2,1]$ is $[-2,2]$.\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (1,5)$ is $(1,3]$.\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n[-2,2] \\cup (1,3] = [-2,3]\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186179479 1074 "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-2,1]$ is $[-2,2].$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (1,5)$ is $(1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[-2,2] \\cup (1,3] = [-2,3].$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179650 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508038 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179650 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508051 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508049 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508055 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508089 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186179878 1050 420.0 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186180785 1064 "The range of a function can be thought of as the set of $y$-values given by the function's graph. For that reason, it's often helpful to plot the function when determining its range.\n\nFor example, consider the linear function\n\n$$\nf(x) =3-2x\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any real number. What is the range of $f(x)$?\n\nSince this is a linear function, we only need two points to plot it. For example:\n\n- When $x=0$, we have $y=3$. This gives the point $(0,3)$.\n- When $x=1$, we have $y=1$. This gives the point $(1,1)$.\n\nPlotting these two points and connecting them, we obtain the following diagram.\n\n![](images/51/5124e487118191ae40248d4488450d065fe71712f709ac62321d749ac17b0127.png)\n\nNotice that the graph $y=f(x)$ will cover all possible $y$-values, so the range of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\infty < y < \\infty\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can also write the range as $(-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\nLet's now look at an example of a linear function with a restricted domain." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186180785 1064 "The range of a function can be thought of as the set of $y$-values given by the function's graph. For that reason, it's often helpful to plot the function when determining its range.\n\nFor example, consider the linear function \n\n$$\nf(x) =3-2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any real number. What is the range of $f(x)$?\n\nSince this is a linear function, we only need two points to plot it. For example:\n\n- When $x=0,$ we have $y=3.$ This gives the point $(0,3).$\n\n- When $x=1,$ we have $y=1.$ This gives the point $(1,1).$\n\nPlotting these two points and connecting them, we obtain the following diagram.\n\n![](images/51/5124e487118191ae40248d4488450d065fe71712f709ac62321d749ac17b0127.png)\n\nNotice that the graph $y=f(x)$ will cover all possible $y$-values, so the range of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\infty <          y <          \\infty.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can also write the range as $(-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nLet's now look at an example of a linear function with a restricted domain." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186184877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183871 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186198365 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508061 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186198406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508048 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186198407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508098 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186200933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186200934 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186200933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186200935 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186200933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186200936 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186200933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186200937 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186200933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186200938 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186200933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507669 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186200933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507710 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [1759218620093