^{2} + 15w + 25 &=  \\\\\n2w^{2} + 5w + 10w + 25 &=  \\\\\n2w^{2} + 10w + 5w + 25 &=  \\\\\n(2w^{2} + 10w) + (5w + 25) &=  \\\\\n2w(w + 5) + 5(w + 5) &=  \\\\\n&= (2w + 5)(w + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n-2(2w + 5)(w + 5)\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186203744 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-4w^2-30w-50\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms in the trinomial have a common factor of $-2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n-2(2w^2+15w+25)\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $2w^2+15w+25$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 25 = 50$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $50.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $50$ are\n\n$$\n(1,50),\\quad (2,25),\\quad (5,10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,10)$ add to $15{:}$ \n\n$$\n5+10 =15  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5w+10w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}+15w+25 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+5w+10w+25 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+10w+5w+25 & = \\\\ (2{w}^{2}+10w)+(5w+25) & = \\\\ 2w(w+5)+5(w+5) & = \\\\ (2w+5)(w+5) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(2w+5)(w+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186203745 1071 "Factor the trinomial $-10t^2 - 35t + 20$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186203745 1071 "Factor the trinomial $-10t^2 - 35t + 20.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186203745 1074 "We must factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-10t^2 - 35t +20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-5.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(2{t}^{2}+7t-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2t^2 + 7t - 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times (-4) = -8$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-8$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-8),\\quad (-1,8),\\quad (2,-4),\\quad (-2,4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,8)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n-1+8 = 8-1 = 7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-t+8t,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+7t-4 & = \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-t+8t-4 & = \\\\ (2{t}^{2}-t)+(8t-4) & = \\\\ t(2t-1)+4(2t-1) & = \\\\ (2t-1)(t+4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(2t-1)(t+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186203745 1074 "We must factor the following expression:\n$-10t^2 - 35t +20$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-5$. So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-5$:\n\n$$\n-5(2t^{2} + 7t - 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2t^2 + 7t - 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times (-4) = -8$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-8$ are\n$(1,-8),\\quad (-1,8),\\quad (2,-4),\\quad (-2,4)$.\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,8)$ add to $7{:}$\n\n$$\n-1+8 = 8-1 = 7 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-t+8t$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t^{2} + 7t - 4 &=  \\\\\n2t^{2} - t + 8t - 4 &=  \\\\\n(2t^{2} - t) + (8t - 4) &=  \\\\\nt(2t - 1) + 4(2t - 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (2t - 1)(t + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n$-5(2t - 1)(t + 4)$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186203746 1071 "Factor the trinomial $15x^2-40x+20$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186203746 1071 "Factor the trinomial $15x^2-40x+20.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186203746 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n15x^2-40x+20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $5$. So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $5{:}$\n$5(3x^{2} - 8x + 4)$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $3x^2 - 8x + 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $3 \\times 4 = 12$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-8$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $12$.\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\quad (-2,-6),\\quad (-3,-4)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-2,-6)$ add to $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n-2-6= -8 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-2x-6x$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 8x + 4 &=  \\\\\n3x^{2} - 2x - 6x + 4 &=  \\\\\n3x^{2} - 6x - 2x + 4 &=  \\\\\n(3x^{2} - 6x) - (2x - 4) &=  \\\\\n3x(x - 2) - 2(x - 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (3x - 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n$5(3x - 2)(x - 2)$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186203746 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n15x^2-40x+20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $5.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3{x}^{2}-8x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $3x^2 - 8x + 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $3 \\times 4 = 12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-8$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\quad (-2,-6),\\quad (-3,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-2,-6)$ add to $-8{:}$ \n\n$$\n-2-6= -8  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-2x-6x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-8x+4 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2x-6x+4 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6x-2x+4 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6x)-(2x-4) & = \\\\ 3x(x-2)-2(x-2) & = \\\\ (3x-2)(x-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3x-2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186203852 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\n- $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\n- $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186203852 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nI. $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\nII. $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\nIII. $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186203942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508114 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186203943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508054 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186203943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508073 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186203944 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508106 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508100 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508071 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508045 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508108 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204138 1050 900.0 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204177 1050 600.0 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204336 1071 "Factor $6x^{2} + 26x + 8$." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186204336 1071 "Factor $6{x}^{2}+26x+8.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204336 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+26x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n2(3{x}^{2}+13x+4)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $3{x}^{2}+13x+4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3\\times 4=12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($13$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(1,12),\\,(3,4),\\,(6,2).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,12)$ add to $13:$ \n\n$$\n1+12=13\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $x+12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+13x+4= \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+x+12x+4 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+x)+(12x+4) & = \\\\ x(3x+1)+4(3x+1) & = \\\\ (x+4)(3x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)(3x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204337 1071 "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.\n\n$8{y}^{2}-36y+40=$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$y-5)(y\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204337 1071 "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.$8y^{2} - 36y + 40 =$\n{{field-1}} $($\n{{field-2}} $y - 5)(y -$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186204337 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n8{y}^{2}-36y+40\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $4.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2{y}^{2}-9y+10$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2\\times 10=20$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-9$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $20.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $20$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-20),\\,(-2,-10),\\,(-4,-5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-5)$ add to $-9:$ \n\n$$\n-4-5=-9\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4y-5y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-9y+10 & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y-5y+10 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}-4y)-(5y-10) & = \\\\ 2y(y-2)-5(y-2) & = \\\\ (2y-5)(y-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}^{2}-36y+40 & = \\\\ 4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10) & = \\\\ 4(2y-5)(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204596 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x$, given by\n$2px = 3$\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p$?" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186204596 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2px=3\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186204596 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2p=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=0.\n$$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186205207 1064 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with $b \\neq 0$ has no solutions when $a = 0.$\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7q & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7q}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186205207 1064 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with $b \\neq 0$ has no solutions when $a = 0.$\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7q & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7q}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**\n\nSimilarly, the equation\n\n\\[\n(p + 3)x = -9,\n\\]\n\nwhere [math]p[/math] is a constant, is an equation in the form [math]ax = b{:}[/math] \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(p + 3)}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-9}_{\\large b},\n\\]\n\nIn this case,\n\n\\[\na = p + 3, \\qquad b = -9.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\\[\np + 3 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving this for [math]p,[/math] we get\n\\begin{align*}\np + 3 & = 0 \\\\[5pt]\np & = -3.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the equation has no solution when [math]p=-3.[/math]" 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186205291 1064 "When factoring a trinomial, we should always begin by factoring out any constant factors. Then, we factor whatever quadratic remains using the techniques we've studied.\n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n6y^2+21y+9\n$$\n\nThe first thing to notice is that all the terms have a common factor of $3$. So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $3$:\n$3(2y^2 + 7y + 3)$\n\nSo, our goal now is to factor the trinomial in parentheses:\n\n$$\n2y^2 + 7y + 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 3 = 6$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nAlso, since all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the *positive* factor pairs of $6$.\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6),\\quad (2,3)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,6)$ add to $7{:}$\n\n$$\n1+6 =7 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $y+6y$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y^{2} + 7y + 3 = \\\\\n2y^{2} + y + 6y + 3 &=  \\\\\n(2y^{2} + y) + (6y + 3) &=  \\\\\ny(2y + 1) + 3(2y + 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (y + 3)(2y + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6y^{2} + 21y + 9 &=  \\\\\n3(2y^{2} + 7y + 3) &=  \\\\\n&= 3(y + 3)(2y + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWhenever the coefficient of the quadratic term is negative, we should always factor this out first. That way, we're left with the task of factoring a trinomial containing a positive quadratic coefficient. Let's see an example." 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [17592186205291 1064 "When factoring a trinomial, we should always begin by factoring out any constant factors. Then, we factor whatever quadratic remains using the techniques we've studied. \n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n6y^2+21y+9\n$$\n\nThe first thing to notice is that all the terms have a common factor of $3.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3(2y^2 + 7y + 3)\n$$\n\nSo, our goal now is to factor the trinomial in parentheses:\n\n$$\n2y^2 + 7y + 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 3 = 6$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nAlso, since all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the *positive* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6),\\quad (2,3).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,6)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n1+6 =7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $y+6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}+7y+3= \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+y+6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}+y)+(6y+3) & = \\\\ y(2y+1)+3(2y+1) & = \\\\ (y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}+21y+9 & = \\\\ 3(2{y}^{2}+7y+3) & = \\\\ 3(y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWhenever the coefficient of the quadratic term is negative, we should always factor this out first. That way, we're left with the task of factoring a trinomial containing a positive quadratic coefficient. Let's see an example." 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186231418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508036 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186235012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507825 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186235012 1078 #edb/ref 17592186234727 13194139535458 17592186045417 false] [1759218623501